import { BlogArticlePage } from "../../BlogArticlePage";

const html = "\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen Dictation is not working on your Mac, whether a MacBook Air, a MacBook Pro or a desktop Mac, the cause is usually a setting, the microphone or a stuck speech service, and what you see on screen tells you which. Start with these three quick fixes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"blog-tldr\" aria-label=\"In short\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"blog-tldr-title\"\u003EIn short\u003C/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cul\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETurn Dictation off and on\u003C/strong\u003E in System Settings \u003E Keyboard \u003E Dictation.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChoose the built-in microphone\u003C/strong\u003E as the microphone source in the same section.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERestart the speech service:\u003C/strong\u003E run \u003Ccode\u003Ekillall corespeechd\u003C/code\u003E in Terminal. No password needed.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis guide is sorted by symptom. Before writing it, we tested the speech service restart and offline dictation on a MacBook Pro with M5 running macOS Tahoe 26.3, on November 12, 2026. Everything else is based on Apple's own documentation, linked where it is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"try-these-three-fixes-first\"\u003ETry these three fixes first\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E1. Turn Dictation off and on.\u003C/strong\u003E Open System Settings, click Keyboard, scroll to Dictation and switch it off. Wait a few seconds, switch it on again and click Enable if macOS asks. This reloads the Dictation setup without touching anything else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E2. Check the microphone source.\u003C/strong\u003E In the same Dictation section, the \"Microphone source\" menu shows which device your Mac listens to. \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/use-dictation-mh40584/mac\"\u003EApple explains\u003C/a\u003E that Automatic picks the device you are most likely to use. That guess is sometimes AirPods or another Bluetooth headset that is still connected, or a display without a microphone. Pick the built-in microphone to rule this out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cfigure\u003E\u003Cimg src=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-keyboard-dictation-settings-bc0a6bb9f8-680.webp\" srcset=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-keyboard-dictation-settings-bc0a6bb9f8-680.webp 680w, /blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-keyboard-dictation-settings-bc0a6bb9f8-960.webp 960w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 768px) 680px, calc(100vw - 40px)\" width=\"680\" height=\"479\" alt=\"The Dictation section of macOS Keyboard settings with Languages, Microphone source, Shortcut and Auto-punctuation\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"\u003E\u003Cfigcaption\u003EThe Dictation settings on our test Mac with macOS 26.3. Microphone source and Shortcut are the two menus to check first.\u003Ca class=\"blog-image-full\" href=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-keyboard-dictation-settings-bc0a6bb9f8-960.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open full-size image (new tab): The Dictation section of macOS Keyboard settings with Languages, Microphone source, Shortcut and Auto-punctuation\"\u003EView full-size image\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/figcaption\u003E\u003C/figure\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E3. Restart the speech service.\u003C/strong\u003E Open Terminal and run:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"blog-code\"\u003E\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ekillall corespeechd\n\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\u003Cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"blog-code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy code\" data-copied-label=\"Copied\"\u003ECopy\u003C/button\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cfigure\u003E\u003Cimg src=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-terminal-restart-corespeechd-26c0c2f9c6-680.webp\" srcset=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-terminal-restart-corespeechd-26c0c2f9c6-680.webp 680w, /blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-terminal-restart-corespeechd-26c0c2f9c6-1000.webp 1000w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 768px) 680px, calc(100vw - 40px)\" width=\"680\" height=\"178\" alt=\"Terminal output: pgrep shows corespeechd as process 85153, killall corespeechd stops it, and pgrep then shows a new process 36843\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"\u003E\u003Cfigcaption\u003EOn our test Mac, corespeechd ran as process 85153. After killall, macOS started it again right away as process 36843.\u003Ca class=\"blog-image-full\" href=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-terminal-restart-corespeechd-26c0c2f9c6-1000.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open full-size image (new tab): Terminal output: pgrep shows corespeechd as process 85153, killall corespeechd stops it, and pgrep then shows a new process 36843\"\u003EView full-size image\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/figcaption\u003E\u003C/figure\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn our test, this needed no administrator password, and macOS started a fresh \u003Ccode\u003Ecorespeechd\u003C/code\u003E process 0.19 seconds later. Dictation worked on the next try. Some guides recommend \u003Ccode\u003Esudo killall -9 corespeechd\u003C/code\u003E. On macOS 26.3 the plain command was enough, because \u003Ccode\u003Ecorespeechd\u003C/code\u003E runs under your own user account. A separate system process, \u003Ccode\u003Ecorespeechd_system\u003C/code\u003E, is not touched by it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-your-symptom\"\u003EFind your symptom\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ctable\u003E\n\u003Cthead\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003EWhat you see\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003EMost likely cause\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003EWhere to look\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003C/thead\u003E\n\u003Ctbody\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003ENothing happens when you press the shortcut\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EShortcut changed, or the key is assigned to something else\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"#nothing-happens-when-you-press-the-dictation-shortcut\"\u003EThe shortcut does nothing\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003ESiri opens instead of Dictation\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EMicrophone key held instead of pressed\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"#nothing-happens-when-you-press-the-dictation-shortcut\"\u003EThe shortcut does nothing\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EMicrophone icon appears, but no text\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EWrong microphone, low input level, stuck speech service\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"#the-microphone-icon-appears-but-no-text-is-typed\"\u003EIcon but no text\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EDictation switch is missing or grayed out\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EVoice Control is on, or a restriction applies\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"#dictation-is-grayed-out-or-missing\"\u003EGrayed out\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EDictation stops by itself\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E30 seconds without speech\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"#dictation-stops-after-a-pause\"\u003EStops by itself\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EWrong words or wrong language\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EDictation language, noise\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"#wrong-words-or-the-wrong-language\"\u003EWrong words\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003C/tbody\u003E\n\u003C/table\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nothing-happens-when-you-press-the-dictation-shortcut\"\u003ENothing happens when you press the Dictation shortcut\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart with the shortcut itself. In Keyboard settings, the Dictation section has a Shortcut menu, and it may not be the key you remember. Apple notes that choosing a shortcut can also change the \"Press fn key to\" option, so a key that used to start Dictation may now do something else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn our test Mac, the shortcut was the Microphone key, while the Globe key was set to \"Show Emoji &#x26; Symbols\" in the same Keyboard settings. Pressing Globe there opens the emoji picker, not Dictation. If you are used to the Globe key, check both settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your keyboard has a Microphone key, the difference between pressing and holding matters. According to \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/use-dictation-mh40584/mac\"\u003EApple\u003C/a\u003E, pressing and releasing it starts Dictation, while pressing and holding it activates Siri. If Siri keeps showing up, tap the key instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeyboard remapping tools, such as Karabiner-Elements, can also intercept the key before macOS sees it. A quick test that skips the shortcut entirely: open TextEdit and choose Edit \u003E Start Dictation from the menu bar. If that works, Dictation is fine and the problem is the key.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-microphone-icon-appears-but-no-text-is-typed\"\u003EThe microphone icon appears, but no text is typed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe icon means Dictation started, so the problem is in what it hears or in the speech service.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck the microphone source first, as described above. Then open System Settings \u003E Sound \u003E Input, speak and watch the input level. If the meter barely moves, raise the input volume or move closer. \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/if-dictation-on-mac-doesnt-work-as-expected-mchlc480652b/mac\"\u003EApple also suggests\u003C/a\u003E checking that nothing covers the built-in microphone and avoiding rooms with a lot of echo or background noise.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cfigure\u003E\u003Cimg src=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-sound-input-level-a11f41baff-680.webp\" srcset=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-sound-input-level-a11f41baff-680.webp 680w, /blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-sound-input-level-a11f41baff-960.webp 960w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 768px) 680px, calc(100vw - 40px)\" width=\"680\" height=\"133\" alt=\"The Input volume slider and the Input level meter in macOS Sound settings, with eight of fifteen level bars lit\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"\u003E\u003Cfigcaption\u003EInput level in Sound settings while a test sentence played on our Mac. Lit bars mean the microphone hears sound.\u003Ca class=\"blog-image-full\" href=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-sound-input-level-a11f41baff-960.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open full-size image (new tab): The Input volume slider and the Input level meter in macOS Sound settings, with eight of fifteen level bars lit\"\u003EView full-size image\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/figcaption\u003E\u003C/figure\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the level is fine and still no text appears, restart the speech service with \u003Ccode\u003Ekillall corespeechd\u003C/code\u003E. When we watched the processes on our test Mac, two more appeared only while Dictation was active, \u003Ccode\u003Elocalspeechrecognition\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Ecom.apple.siri.embeddedspeech\u003C/code\u003E. They start on demand, so a missing process in Activity Monitor is not a problem by itself when Dictation is off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dictation-is-grayed-out-or-missing\"\u003EDictation is grayed out or missing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe most common reason is Voice Control. \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/use-dictation-mh40584/mac\"\u003EApple states\u003C/a\u003E that when Voice Control is on, you dictate through Voice Control and standard macOS Dictation is not available. Check System Settings \u003E Accessibility \u003E Voice Control and turn it off if you want the regular Dictation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cfigure\u003E\u003Cimg src=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-voice-control-setting-e40097f369-680.webp\" srcset=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-voice-control-setting-e40097f369-680.webp 680w, /blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-voice-control-setting-e40097f369-960.webp 960w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 768px) 680px, calc(100vw - 40px)\" width=\"680\" height=\"276\" alt=\"The Voice Control setting in macOS Accessibility settings, switched off\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"\u003E\u003Cfigcaption\u003EVoice Control in Accessibility settings. While this switch is on, the regular Dictation is not available.\u003Ca class=\"blog-image-full\" href=\"/blog/media/dictation-not-working-on-mac-voice-control-setting-e40097f369-960.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open full-size image (new tab): The Voice Control setting in macOS Accessibility settings, switched off\"\u003EView full-size image\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/figcaption\u003E\u003C/figure\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo more things are worth checking:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScreen Time.\u003C/strong\u003E Under Screen Time \u003E Content &#x26; Privacy, Siri and related features can be restricted. If someone set up Screen Time on your Mac, look there.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA managed Mac.\u003C/strong\u003E On a Mac from work or school, an administrator can restrict features through device management. If the switch is locked, the IT team is the right contact.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDictation is also not offered in every language or region. Apple lists the supported languages on its \u003Ca href=\"https://www.apple.com/macos/feature-availability/#dictation\"\u003EmacOS feature availability page\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dictation-stops-after-a-pause\"\u003EDictation stops after a pause\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis one is by design. \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/use-dictation-mh40584/mac\"\u003EApple documents\u003C/a\u003E that you can dictate text of any length without a timeout, but Dictation stops automatically when no speech is detected for 30 seconds. If you pause to think, start it again with the shortcut.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac with Apple silicon, you can also type while Dictation is running. Apple says the cursor stops pulsing while you type and resumes when you stop, so you do not need to end Dictation to fix a word.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wrong-words-or-the-wrong-language\"\u003EWrong words or the wrong language\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the Dictation section in Keyboard settings and click Edit next to Languages. Make sure the language you speak is selected. If you set up more than one, you can switch while dictating by clicking the language shown next to the cursor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor single words, Apple underlines ambiguous results in blue. Click the underlined word to pick an alternative. Numbers and times are formatted for you: in our test, \"seven fifteen\" became \"7:15\" and \"platform four\" became \"platform 4\".\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"does-mac-dictation-need-the-internet\"\u003EDoes Mac Dictation need the internet?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt depends on the language and the text field. \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/use-dictation-mh40584/mac\"\u003EApple says\u003C/a\u003E the text below the Dictation switch in Keyboard settings tells you whether your voice input is processed on your Mac. Search boxes are an exception and can still use Apple's servers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThat text is worth reading closely. On our test Mac it said: \"Dictation sends information like your voice input, contacts and location to Apple to process your requests.\" So we checked what happens without a connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe tested it. On our MacBook Pro with M5 and macOS 26.3, English (US and UK) and German were installed for on-device use. We turned Wi-Fi off, confirmed that apple.com could not be reached, and dictated a sentence into TextEdit. It came out as \"The train to Hamburg leaves at 7:15 from platform 4.\" That shows on-device Dictation working in TextEdit without a connection. We did not test search fields or other apps offline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-separate-dictation-app-makes-sense\"\u003EWhen a separate dictation app makes sense\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf Apple Dictation works for you after these fixes, there is no need to install anything else. A separate app is worth a look when you want to choose the speech model yourself, need languages Apple does not offer on-device, or want to see in the source code what happens to your voice. \u003Ca href=\"/\"\u003Ehush\u003C/a\u003E is one such app. It is free and open source during the alpha, and once the models are downloaded, transcription runs on your Mac. Model downloads and updates need an internet connection. hush is not affiliated with Apple.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\" class=\"blog-appendix\"\u003EFrequently asked questions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"why-has-my-dictation-stopped-working-on-my-mac\"\u003EWhy has my dictation stopped working on my Mac?\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost often the microphone source changed, the shortcut changed, or the speech service is stuck. Turn Dictation off and on in Keyboard settings, select the built-in microphone, and run \u003Ccode\u003Ekillall corespeechd\u003C/code\u003E in Terminal.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"how-do-i-reset-the-microphone-on-my-mac\"\u003EHow do I reset the microphone on my Mac?\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no single reset button. Select the microphone again under System Settings \u003E Sound \u003E Input and as the microphone source in the Dictation settings. Then check that the input level moves when you speak.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"why-does-voice-typing-not-work-in-chrome-or-google-docs\"\u003EWhy does voice typing not work in Chrome or Google Docs?\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart Dictation in TextEdit first. If it works there, macOS Dictation is fine, and the issue is with the website or browser. Google Docs also has its own \u003Ca href=\"https://support.google.com/docs/answer/4492226\"\u003Evoice typing feature\u003C/a\u003E, which is separate from Apple Dictation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"does-mac-dictation-work-offline\"\u003EDoes Mac Dictation work offline?\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor languages that are installed on your Mac, it can. In our test on macOS 26.3, Dictation in TextEdit worked with Wi-Fi turned off, even though the Keyboard settings said Dictation sends voice input to Apple. Search boxes and other apps may behave differently.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sources-and-how-we-tested\" class=\"blog-appendix\"\u003ESources and how we tested\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003EApple Support, \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/use-dictation-mh40584/mac\"\u003EDictate messages and documents on Mac\u003C/a\u003E. Accessed November 12, 2026.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003EApple Support, \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/if-dictation-on-mac-doesnt-work-as-expected-mchlc480652b/mac\"\u003EIf Dictation on Mac doesn't work as expected\u003C/a\u003E. Accessed November 12, 2026.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003EApple Support, \u003Ca href=\"https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/block-features-or-content-with-screen-time-mchl8490d51e/mac\"\u003EBlock features or content with Screen Time on Mac\u003C/a\u003E. Accessed November 12, 2026.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003EApple, \u003Ca href=\"https://www.apple.com/macos/feature-availability/#dictation\"\u003EmacOS feature availability: Dictation\u003C/a\u003E. Accessed November 12, 2026.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003EGoogle Docs Help, \u003Ca href=\"https://support.google.com/docs/answer/4492226\"\u003EType with your voice\u003C/a\u003E. Accessed November 12, 2026.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHow this guide was prepared:\u003C/strong\u003E We tested the speech service restart, offline dictation and the dictation settings on a MacBook Pro with M5 and 16 GB of memory, running macOS 26.3, on November 12, 2026. The screenshots come from that Mac, cropped to the relevant settings. Fixes we did not test ourselves, such as Screen Time and Voice Control, are based on Apple's documentation above and say so in the text. AI tools assisted with research and drafting; the author checked every claim and step before publishing. hush is our own product, and it is mentioned once, after the fixes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EApple, Mac, macOS and Siri are trademarks of Apple Inc.\u003C/p\u003E";

export function Article() {
  return <BlogArticlePage lang="en" slug="dictation-not-working-on-mac" html={html} />;
}
